codex_new=r'''
## Config isolation (T03 of docs/plans/projects.md)

Probe design: `{P}/setup-codex.sh` builds a world with a temporary `CODEX_HOME` (`home/`, no credentials) and two git folders, `trusted/` (listed as `trust_level = "trusted"` in `home/config.toml`) and `untrusted/` (no entry). Each folder has `.codex/config.toml` with `notify`, `model_provider` + `[model_providers.evil]` (base_url `http://127.0.0.1:1/v1`), `model_instructions_file`, `web_search = "live"`, an MCP server `projmcp` whose command is `/usr/bin/touch <marker>`, and a SessionStart hook that touches a marker; `home/config.toml` has the same `notify`, an MCP server `usermcp` and a hook at user level. `{P}/run-codex.sh` runs `codex exec --sandbox read-only x` from the folder, stdin `/dev/null`, 15 s timeout. Raw output: `{P}/probe-codex-1.txt`, `{P}/c1/out-*.txt`; `codex exec --help`, `codex --help`, `codex exec resume --help`, `codex features list`: `{P}/codex-exec-help.txt`, `codex-help.txt`, `codex-exec-resume-help.txt`, `codex-features.txt`.

Positive control (no candidate): in `trusted/` the markers `trusted-mcp` and `user-mcp` were written (the MCP command is spawned at session start, before the model call fails); in `untrusted/` only `user-mcp`. The two hooks and `notify` wrote nothing. The turn then retried `wss://api.openai.com/v1/responses` and was cut by the timeout (exit 124).

| Candidate | result | reading |
|---|---|---|
| project `.codex/config.toml` in a trusted folder | `trusted-mcp` written | probe, positive control fired: `exec` loads the project config of a trusted folder |
| project `.codex/config.toml` in an untrusted folder | no `untrusted-mcp`, no warning | probe, control fired (the same file in `trusted/` wrote it): the project layer is not loaded; matches "Codex ignores project `.codex/` layers" — ''' + CF + r'''
| keys `notify`, `model_provider`, `model_providers` in a trusted project file | startup warning "Ignored unsupported project-local config keys in .../.codex/config.toml: model_provider, model_providers, notify. If you want these settings to apply, manually set them in your user-level config.toml." (printed twice) | probe: these keys cannot come from a project file at all, so they need no `-c`; the docs list base URL, provider, `notify`, profile and OpenTelemetry settings as ignored in project files — ''' + CF + r'''
| `model_instructions_file`, `web_search` in the project file | no warning | not in the ignored list the warning printed; whether they apply is unverified offline (they redirect the prompt or the tool set, nothing writes a file); override with `-c` on every turn |
| `-c 'mcp_servers.projmcp.enabled=false' -c 'mcp_servers.usermcp.enabled=false'` | no markers | verified (control fired): `enabled=false` per server switches a server off, but only for servers whose name is known; a project can add any name |
| `-c 'mcp_servers={}'` | `trusted-mcp`, `user-mcp` written | does not remove servers: not usable |
| `--ignore-user-config` (trusted folder) | no markers at all | verified (control fired): drops `usermcp`, and also `projmcp`, because the trust entry lives in the user `config.toml` — without it the project layer is untrusted and not loaded. One flag removes user MCP servers and the project layer; help of 0.164.0: "Do not load `$CODEX_HOME/config.toml`; auth still uses `CODEX_HOME`" — help text plus probe |
| `--ignore-user-config` (untrusted folder) | no markers | consistent |
| `--ignore-rules` | `trusted-mcp`, `user-mcp` written | affects nothing else, as its help says: "Do not load user or project execpolicy `.rules` files" — help text |
| `--disable hooks` | `trusted-mcp`, `user-mcp` written | irrelevant to MCP, as expected; the hook markers never fired in any run, so the switch is unverified |
| `-c web_search="disabled"` | no effect observable offline | unverified; the binary's own deprecation text names the values: "Set `web_search` to `"live"`, `"indexed"`, `"cached"`, or `"disabled"` at the top level (or under a profile) in config.toml" (`strings` of the 0.164.0 binary, `{P}/codex-web-search-values.txt`); reference: `--search` "sets `web_search = "live"` instead of the default "cached"" — help text — ''' + CX + r'''

- Hooks: both the user-level and the project-level SessionStart hook wrote nothing in the trusted folder, with no warning on stdout. The docs say why: "Before a non-managed hook can run, Codex requires you to review and trust the exact hook definition" (trust is tied to the hook's hash, reviewed with `/hooks`); "Project-local hooks load only when the project `.codex/` layer is trusted. By contrast, User-level hooks remain independent of project trust"; "Hooks are on by default" and `[features].hooks = false` turns them off — ''' + HK + r''' . No positive control could fire (a hook can run only after trust, and `--dangerously-bypass-hook-trust`, "Run enabled hooks without requiring persisted hook trust for this invocation. DANGEROUS", must never be used, so it was not used even as a control). Result: the default (an untrusted hook does not run) is documented and was observed to hold for both levels, but is unverified by the plan's rule; a room turn never passes the bypass flag and `--ignore-user-config` also removes the user hooks (they live in the user layer) — help text, unverified.
- User-level plugins and MCP servers under `exec`: the user MCP server `usermcp` was started by `exec` (marker `user-mcp`): user MCP servers load under `exec`, and `--ignore-user-config` removes them (probe above). The owner's config lists `[plugins."browser-use@openai-bundled"] enabled = true` and `[marketplaces.*]` entries (keys only read from `~/.codex/config.toml`; no value printed); a plugin cannot be installed in a credential-free temporary home, so whether `exec` loads it and whether `-c 'plugins."browser-use@openai-bundled".enabled=false'` switches it off is unverified; `codex features list` shows `plugins` and `browser_use` as stable and on (`{P}/codex-features.txt`), so `-c features.plugins=false` and `--disable plugins` are the candidates for AC5. With `--ignore-user-config` the `[plugins]` table is not read at all (help text).
- Auth: the temporary home has none, so "auth survives `--ignore-user-config` / `--ignore-rules`" cannot be probed. Help text of 0.164.0: `--ignore-user-config`: "Do not load `$CODEX_HOME/config.toml`; auth still uses `CODEX_HOME`"; `--ignore-rules`: "Do not load user or project execpolicy `.rules` files" (nothing about auth) — help text — ''' + CX + r'''
- Session name: `codex exec` and `codex exec resume` have no name option (`{P}/codex-exec-help.txt`); `exec resume [SESSION_ID]` takes "Conversation/session id (UUID) or thread name. UUIDs take precedence if it parses"; `--last` picks the newest; `--all` "Show all sessions (disables cwd filtering)", so lookup by `--last` or by name is filtered by cwd unless `--all` — help text of `codex exec resume --help`. Setting a thread name from `exec` is not possible on the command line; the equivalent of `claude -n` is absent, so a room names its Codex session only in the app (the room keeps the UUID from the rollout) — help text — ''' + CX + r'''
- Resume per cwd, probe: after one session per folder, `codex exec --sandbox read-only resume --last x` from `trusted/` and from `untrusted/` printed different `session id` values (`01a16b0b-c7c1-...` and `01a16b0c-6418-...`); each folder resumed a session recorded in its own folder, consistent with the cwd filter (the sessions' own `session_meta.cwd` was not compared, so this is weak evidence) — probe — ''' + CX + r'''
- Rollout origin, from a real rollout this probe produced (field names and values only, no content, `{P}/c1/home/sessions/2026/10/24/rollout-*.jsonl`, first record): `{"type":"session_meta","payload":{"session_id","id","timestamp","cwd","runtime_workspace_roots","originator":"codex_exec","cli_version":"0.164.0","source":"exec","thread_source":"user","model_provider","base_instructions","history_mode":"paginated","context_window","git"}}`. So `exec` writes `source: "exec"` and `originator: "codex_exec"`; D27's interactive values (`cli`/`codex-tui`, `vscode`/`codex-tui`) were read from the owner's rollouts in the plan and not re-read here — probe, first record of a 0.164.0 rollout — https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/rust-v0.164.0/codex-rs/rollout/src/recorder.rs
- `exec` header of a run shows the effective config: `approval: never`, `sandbox: read-only`, `model`, `provider: openai`, and `session id` (`{P}/c1/out-trusted-control.txt`); with the project file's `model_provider = "evil"` the provider stayed `openai`, which confirms the ignore warning — probe.
- `AGENTS.md`: not config, so the isolation flags above do not touch it; a project `AGENTS.md` and `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` are read by Codex per its docs (not probed, no prompt visible offline) — https://developers.openai.com/codex/guides/agents-md
'''
